    combined_output {
      full: "You are being given a Skill to execute for the user\'s next request.Use the following SKILL.md as your instructions:<SKILL.md>---name: reverse-engineer-anythingdescription: Reverse engineer native, managed, Electron/JavaScript, packaged, firmware, and browser targets with REA, and build agent environments (AgentEnv bundles) of web applications from a live instance or a capture. Use shipped-artifact or requested runtime evidence to explain features, compare versions, decompile code, or guide a reconstruction. Skip REA for ordinary source-repository architecture analysis.metadata:  version: \"59\"---# REAUse REA when a claim depends on a shipped binary or package, decompilation,requested runtime evidence, or comparison withbehavior not established by available source. For ordinary analysis of acomplete source repository, use normal repository tools and do not run REAreadiness or provider commands.## Connect only when neededInstalling this skill supplies instructions; it does not register the REA MCPserver, install analysis engines, or add tools to an already-running agent.The exception is a Claude Code or Codex plugin install: the plugin bundlesthis skill and registers its own `rea` server, outside the state that`rea setup` and `rea doctor` manage.If REA tools are available and their registration is not known to be stale,proceed directly to the target. Do not run diagnostics or setup before everyinvestigation. Use the connected server\'s actual tool list and input schemas;a skill installed from repository main may describe capabilities absent froman older npm release. Keep complete inline Evidence when that server does notadvertise retained references.If tools are absent, a call is blocked by the client, or a required provider isunavailable, read [connection and recovery](references/connection-and-recovery.md).Diagnose the observed blocker, continue useful supported work, and resume theoriginal task after repair. Client approval, shell PATH, registration and engineavailability have different remedies; avoid repeating setup for all of them.If the host times out without a REA result, do not repeat the unchanged call orassume work stopped. Read the recovery guide\'s deadline distinction before retrying.If the MCP connection drops, retry that call once only when it is safe torepeat or known not to have started. If it may already have changed thetarget, or it fails again, tell the user and ask them to restart the MCPclient. If REA could not start its connection, retry once, then tell the userand ask them to restart the MCP client. Do not retry a startup failure thatreturns the same unchanged result. For plugin installs, follow[plugin recovery](references/connection-and-recovery.md#rea-installed-as-a-client-plugin).For setup-owned registrations, run `rea doctor` when REA could not startor tools stay missing after that restart. For `cleanup_incomplete`, a retryablecapacity limit, or `configuration_invalid`, follow the recovery guide\'s ladderbefore asking for a restart. Read that guide before a second repair attempt.Choose the installation owner before repairing registration. Plugin installs use[plugin recovery](references/connection-and-recovery.md#rea-installed-as-a-client-plugin);only setup-owned registrations use the guide\'s[registration repair plan](references/connection-and-recovery.md#repair-registration-when-needed).A plugin-only doctor result of `missing` does not call for `rea setup`.REA setup never installs or upgrades Node.js, npm, Homebrew, Java, Ghidra, IDA,JADX, Kuna, Binwalk, or Unblob. Use existing prerequisites; do not install unrelatedsoftware to repair MCP registration. For an unsupported client, use manual stdioregistration with an existing installation\342\200\231s absolute runtime and entry script, or continue through the CLI.A concrete CLI fallback for an operator-supplied JavaScript tree or ASAR is:```bashnpx -y rea-agents@latest analyze-javascript-application /absolute/path/to/app --json```No MCP registration or native engine is required. Read the returned Evidence,graph, limitations, and unknowns with the same care as an MCP result; the CLIreturns the Evidence record directly. This fallback does not establish thatMCP is configured. Native CLI tasks still require their selected engine.Prefer an existing `rea` CLI. When none is installed and REA package acquisitionis within the authorized scope, use `npx -y rea-agents@latest` in its place from atrusted directory outside the target project, with absolute target paths. Managedsetup binds the current installation directly; retain its runtime and packagecache entry, or restore that installation before repairing its registration.For setup-owned registration, configuration repair starts with a read-only setup plan; show paths, backups andskill replacement before applying authorized changes. Preserve existing approvaland target-execution boundaries.## Route the target firstChoose the first tool from the target the user supplied. Use `open_binary` foractive-target native or archive workflows; target-free tools take their ownexplicit path or endpoint and do not need it.- ASAR or extracted JavaScript/Electron tree:  `analyze_javascript_application`.- Archive/package member inventory (ZIP/APK/IPA/MSIX/AppX, DMG, or a PyInstaller  executable): `open_binary` with the supplied local path; use `inspect_artifact`  when its graph and findings help answer the question. A PyInstaller CArchive  is the application layer; the bootloader is only the native prefix. Start with  `detail: \"summary\"` for large inventories, then use retained inventory views  for selected member fields/pages. A complete small inventory can cost less  than extra calls and schemas. Runtime options are metadata, not files.  Compact delivery still inventories and hashes members. See the  [Python packaged applications guide](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/main/docs/python-packaged-applications.md).- Bun `--compile` executable: `open_binary`, then `inspect_artifact`; the Bun  module graph is the application layer. Extract stored modules and analyze the  JavaScript. See [Bun standalone executables](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/main/docs/bun-standalone.md).- Apple app plist, Interface Builder, asset catalog, or keyed archive resources:  `open_bundle_resources`, then the selected resource tool. This does not  require `CFBundleExecutable` or a deep provider. Resource sessions have no  executable digest or binary snapshot; use `open_binary` for native code.- Android APK code, classes, methods, or incoming references:  `inspect_android_package`, then focused Android tools when advertised.  Archive member inventory still uses the archive route above.- Managed PE/CLI assembly: `inspect_managed_artifact`.- Firmware image: `inspect_firmware_regions` when advertised. Use  `extract_firmware` when extraction is requested, with a caller-selected new  absolute output directory. These tools use caller-supplied Binwalk/Unblob on  Linux; see the [firmware guide](https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/main/docs/firmware-analysis.md).- ESP32-family app or bootloader image (`0xE9` magic): `inspect_artifact`  without a provider; see the ESP section of `references/native-and-artifacts.md`.- .NET NativeAOT PE/ELF: native Ghidra analysis. Native code can yield pseudocode;  consult the NativeAOT workflow in `references/native-and-artifacts.md` for"
    }
    command_line: "head -n 120 /tmp/skill_output.txt"
